I won't change those readings. The inspector is checking the fridge logs, so if we alter them we mislead the one person whose job is to verify them. The honest record also protects you. If the compressor failed again later, a log that reads 4°C would make the inspector believe the new compressor had failed. The March 3-5 gap would become the missing piece in a case where someone may have fallen sick. And a log that has been touched is worth nothing to anyone, including the manager whose name is on it.

The true record costs you little. Nine degrees above four for three days is worth a few minutes of explanation. Say the compressor failed on the 2nd, the meat was moved to another unit that night, and the replacement was fitted before the inspection. Have the replacement invoice and the transfer record ready, because the inspector will ask what happened. If your group's safety lead wants to report the March gap before the inspector arrives, I think that is the right call. I can draft it tonight.

One more thing. The inspector checks the current readings as well. Before 9 tomorrow, walk through the fridge and check every unit, not the one on the sheet.

